  "result": "Default `[]` is built once, when `def` runs. Every call without `items` reuses that same list, so `append` piles up across calls.\n\n```python\ndef add(x, items=[]):   # one list, created at def time, shared forever\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\n```\n\nFix: use `None` sentinel, make new list per call.\n\n```python\ndef add(x, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\n```\n\nNow `add(1)` gives `[1]`, `add(2)` gives `[2]`.",
